00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
day 36  kiev is still held by the ukrainians  president zielenski has come out and  said that russians russia's invasion is  at a turning point  but given what's happening on the ground  do you think it is a turning point well  the the short answer is no um i think  language is key here um although kiev  clearly there's an operational pause  around kier for the fighting but  fighting is continuing in the donbass  region which is over here let's just  circle that so we know that and it's  very very uh determined fighting and  likewise in mariopol uh down to the  south  it's pretty obvious that phase one of um  russia's plan didn't go entirely  according to plan um but i think you've  we must never forget that russia is a  global superpower with 10 times the size  of resources whilst zolensky's language  may well be there to raise morale of his  troops there's also no doubt that  ukraine is still in a very difficult  situation and we've yet to see phase two  of russia's plan kick in we're talking  of phase two russia's deputy defense  minister um has said there's going to be  reduced military activity around kiev  and chad here but we have seen  um let me get that we've seen attacks on  shona have haven't we so what do they  mean by reduced military activity well  first of all i would be extremely  cynical about um any russian statement  about their tactics you do not tell your  enemy what you're about to do um one of  the the most effective ways of uh  prosecuting war is through deception and  history is littered with that so i think  we have to take any statement with a  large pinch of salt and it but it may be  that because of the situation around  kiev um part of this is the russians  getting making a virtue out of necessity  because the reality is they've got  forces that are stalled and therefore  and both sides do not want to see kiev  end up the same way as mario paul  flattened so it looks like the russians  priorities across the donbass and to the  south and therefore by liberating troops  by adopting a more defensive posture you  need significantly fewer resources to do  defense than you do offense would get  allow them to re-brigade their resources  that doesn't mean you stop fighting you  store fire mortars you still fire  rockets you still fire artillery because  you still want to keep the enemies head  down it does provide though the  ukrainians the opportunity because now  that this forces are static the ukraine  can now mount counter-attacks and i  think we've been seeing a little bit  more of that the channel for the donbass  unfortunately if you remember the  resupply convoy for keith 40 mile convoy  real problems for russia  in the donbass region they don't have  those problems you're right on the  russian border so resupplying fuel  people ammunition would make life a lot  easier for the russians and of course  also make it a lot more difficult for  the ukrainians now the head of gchq has  been speaking in australia overnight and  has said that um putin massively  misjudged his own capabilities and that  his advisers because the situation is so  dire they're simply not telling him the  truth do you think that he did misjudge  the capabilities and do you think those  people are not telling putin what the  real story is on the ground well i think  there can be little doubt that putin's  initial ambition of just walking in and  being welcomed over 48 hours was folly  so  there's no doubt that they didn't gain  this properly in the west when we're  doing any of these sorts of scales of  operations there's an intense planning  period for the invasion of iraq you get  military leadership they train they  practice they look at courses of action  what's the most dangerous what's the  most likely and then you have a very  animated conversation between the senior  leadership and the political masters to  make sure everybody understands the  risks involved  and then you do lots of training to make  sure your troops are ready it's pretty  evident none of the troops were ready  for this the logistics supply wasn't  ready for this and they didn't really  have a plan b  so for putin who speaks truth to power  how does putin engage with that process  rather than just having a bunch of yes  men charging around in the west  as churchill famously described  democracy as the least worst option but  we do have the press we have the people  we have the military and the politicians  all engaged in a very animated  conversations that when people  go across and start a campaign they're  ready and they are they understand what  happens when the plan doesn't survive  contact it's pretty evident that's not  the case i think the irony of all of  this of course is that putin um the one  thing he was seeking to avoid is what's  coming to him he was seeking to avoid  ukrainian resurgence of their  nationalism um also nato  much stronger and coherent and likewise  the impact long term on his economy not  what he would have expected

